nged\\n```\\n\\nBefore commit or push, prefer:\\n\\n```bash\\npython3 path/to/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py --staged\\npython3 path/to/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py --changed --base origin/main\\n```\\n\\nFor a specific artifact:\\n\\n```bash\\npython3 path/to/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py report.md paper.tex slides.pdf\\n```\\n\\nUse `--strict-warnings` when the recipient wants warnings to block delivery.\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\n1. Determine scope:\\n   - For generated files, pass the exact output paths.\\n   - For a commit, use `--staged`.\\n   - For a branch or push, use `--changed --base origin/main` or the relevant upstream base.\\n   - For an initial audit, use `--all`.\\n2. Run the gate script.\\n3. Fix all errors. Treat warnings as review prompts unless `--strict-warnings` is required.\\n4. For LaTeX and PDFs, also use project-native validation when available:\\n   - Compile `.tex` with the repo's existing command or `latexmk`/`pdflatex`.\\n   - Open or inspect generated PDFs enough to confirm they are not blank, corrupt, or stale.\\n5. Re-run the gate after edits.\\n\\n## Human Review Gates\\n\\nThe script catches mechanical issues; still review these before sharing.\\n\\nMarkdown:\\n- Clear title and section hierarchy.\\n- No unresolved placeholders, merge markers, stale TODOs, or fake links.\\n- Tables, lists, math, and fenced code render plausibly.\\n- Claims with external facts have sources or are clearly marked as assumptions.\\n\\nLaTeX:\\n- The file compiles, or the reason it was not compiled is reported.\\n- No unresolved citations/references in the build log when a PDF is produced.\\n- No obvious placeholder text, broken environments, or empty `\\\\ref{}` / `\\\\cite{}` keys.\\n- Generated PDFs correspond to the current `.tex`, not an older build artifact.\\n\\nPDF:\\n- File opens, has at least one page, and is not zero-byte/corrupt.\\n- Expected text is visible/extractable unless the PDF is intentionally image-only.\\n- No accidental blank pages, cropped content, or stale draft/watermark text.\\n\\n## Integration\\n\\nFor repo hooks, CI, and copy/paste setup, read `references/repo-integration.md`.\\nTemplates live in `assets/`.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'if [[ -n ${HERDR_PANE_ID:-} ]]; then herdr pane rename \\\"$HERDR_PANE_ID\\\" \\\"Q7P1 paper drafting\\\"; else print '\\\"'HERDR_PANE_ID unset'; fi\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'if [[ -n ${HERDR_PANE_ID:-} ]]; then herdr pane rename \\\"$HERDR_PANE_ID\\\" \\\"Q7P1 paper drafting\\\"; else print '\\\"'HERDR_PANE_ID unset'; fi\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"HERDR_PANE_ID unset\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"pwd && rg --files -g '\\\"'!*'\\\"' -g 'Q7P1.tex' -g 'Q7P1.verdict.json' -g 'ledger.jsonl' -g 'inputs/**' -g 'ada/**' -g 'emmy/**' -g 'paper/**' -g 'AGENTS.md' | sort\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"pwd && rg --files -g '\\\"'!*'\\\"' -g 'Q7P1.tex' -g 'Q7P1.verdict.json' -g 'ledger.jsonl' -g 'inputs/**' -g 'ada/**' -g 'emmy/**' -g 'paper/**' -g 'AGENTS.md' | sort\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/repeat/threads/Q7P1\\nQ7P1.tex\\nQ7P1.verdict.json\\nledger.jsonl\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I\u2019m applying the style-gates skill because the deliverables include TeX, Markdown, and a PDF. The Herdr pane ID is unavailable in this shell, so I couldn\u2019t verify a pane rename.\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat Q7P1.tex'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat Q7P1.tex'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"```latex\\n\\\\documentclass{article}\\n\\\\usepackage{pathfinder-note}\\n\\n\\\\title{A CVaR Oracle for a Balanced Allocation Game}\\n\\n\\\\begin{document}\\n\\\\maketitle\\n\\n\\\\section{The pair}\\n\\nQ designs quadratic payments for a resource allocation game with separate agent decisions and a shared capacity constraint. P studies sampled conditional value at risk (CVaR) optimization over a communication network in which every agent maintains a copy of the same decision. \\\\ledger{1, 3}\\n\\n\\\\section{Connection pursued}\\n\\nThe question was whether P's black-box CVaR estimates could supply risk-adjusted valuations for Q's incentive game, with guarantees for the true CVaR objective. P's consensus iteration cannot be transferred directly: consensus is appropriate for its shared decision, whereas Q's welfare optimum can require unequal allocations. P's assumption that the feasible set contains a ball centered at zero also cannot be copied literally to Q's nonnegative allocation sets. The reusable ingredient is the local empirical-CVaR estimate. \\\\ledger{3, 20}\\n\\n\\\\section{What was found}\\n\\n\\\\begin{objection}\\nQ's stated strict uniqueness certificate is incompatible with its implementation conditions. P2(i) requires \\\\(\\\\sum_m A_{nm}^n=0\\\\). In the direction with zero allocation change and the same nonzero price change for every agent, this makes the quadratic form of Q's pseudogradient bound zero, contradicting P1(ii)'s strict negative bound. Thus Q's proposed payment cannot satisfy both stated LMIs. This does not, by itself, rule out implementation. \\\\ledger{5, 6}\\n\\\\end{objection}\\n\\nFor Q's payment in Eq.~(40), implementation has a direct argument. Write \\\\(P^k=\\\\sum_n p_n^k\\\\) and \\\\(z^k=\\\\sum_n x_n^k-c^k\\\\). Its price best response satisfies\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{\\\\partial U_n}{\\\\partial p_n^k}\\n=-\\\\frac{\\\\alpha}{N-1}(Np_n^k-P^k-z^k).\\n\\\\]\\nIf one price in coordinate \\\\(k\\\\) is positive, every price there must be positive and equal, and summing the interior conditions gives \\\\(z^k=0\\\\). If all prices vanish, the boundary conditions give \\\\(z^k\\\\leq0\\\\). Hence every equilibrium has a common price \\\\(p\\\\), feasible allocations, and \\\\(p^\\\\top(\\\\sum_nx_n-c)=0\\\\). At that price, each allocation best response maximizes \\\\(V_n(x_n)-\\\\alpha p^\\\\top x_n\\\\), exactly the planner's condition with multiplier \\\\(\\\\lambda=\\\\alpha p\\\\). Under Q's strong concavity and existence of a planner multiplier, the converse follows from joint concavity of each player's payoff. The allocation is unique, but the multiplier and equilibrium price need not be. \\\\ledger{8, 9, 14}\\n\\nFor example, with two agents, one resource, \\\\(c=2\\\\), \\\\(\\\\mathcal X_n=[0,1]\\\\), \\\\(\\\\alpha=1\\\\), and \\\\(V_n(x)=2x-x^2/2\\\\), the allocation \\\\((1,1)\\\\) is an equilibrium with any common price in \\\\([0,1]\\\\). Q's unique-message claim therefore fails. Its original payment can also violate individual rationality: with \\\\(c=1\\\\), \\\\(V_1(x)=4x-x^2/2\\\\), and \\\\(V_2(x)=x-x^2/2\\\\), the equilibrium \\\\((x_1,x_2,p_1,p_2)=(1,0,3,3)\\\\) charges agent 1 \\\\(9/2\\\\), exceeding \\\\(V_1(1)=7/2\\\\). Both examples can be realized by bounded, convex stochastic losses of the type allowed in P. \\\\ledger{7, 10}\\n\\nThe ledger gives an others-only payment correction. Let\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\kappa_N=1+\\\\frac{N}{(N-1)^2},\\n\\\\qquad\\ng_n(s_{-n})=\\n\\\\frac{\\\\alpha N}{(N-1)^3}\\n\\\\bar p_{-n}^{\\\\top}\\n\\\\left(\\\\bar x_{-n}-\\\\frac{N-1}{N}c\\\\right),\\n\\\\]\\nand replace Q's payment \\\\(t_n\\\\) by \\\\(t_n+g_n\\\\). Since \\\\(g_n\\\\) contains no message from agent \\\\(n\\\\), best responses and equilibria are unchanged. Substitution at a common-price, complementary equilibrium gives\\n\\\\[\\nt_n=\\\\kappa_N\\\\lambda^\\\\top(x_n-c/N),\\n\\\\qquad\\nt_n+g_n=\\\\lambda^\\\\top(x_n-c/N).\\n\\\\]\\nThe corrected payments sum to zero. If \\\\(0\\\\in\\\\mathcal X_n\\\\) and \\\\(V_n(0)=0\\\\), the allocation best response also gives\\n\\\\[\\nV_n(x_n)-\\\\lambda^\\\\top x_n+\\\\lambda^\\\\top c/N\\\\geq0,\\n\\\\]\\nso the corrected mechanism is individually rational at every equilibrium. \\\\ledger{12, 13, 14, 24}\\n\\nFor the CVaR connection, let \\\\(C_n(x)\\\\) be agent \\\\(n\\\\)'s true CVaR loss and \\\\(H_n(x)\\\\) its expected empirical CVaR after ball smoothing with radius \\\\(\\\\delta_n\\\\) and a fresh batch of size \\\\(s_n\\\\). P's bounded-loss CVaR comparison and integrated empirical-distribution bound give the pointwise estimate\\n\\\\[\\n|H_n(x)-C_n(x)|\\\\leq h_n,\\n\\\\qquad\\nh_n=L_n\\\\delta_n+\\n\\\\frac{U_n\\\\sqrt{2\\\\pi}}{\\\\beta_n\\\\sqrt{s_n}},\\n\\\\]\\nprovided the perturbation queries are available on the required neighborhood. Form true and surrogate valuations by subtracting \\\\(C_n\\\\) and \\\\(H_n\\\\), respectively, from the same strongly concave utility term, then normalize each valuation at zero. The surrogate valuations retain the required strong concavity. \\\\ledger{15, 17, 24, 26}\\n\\nAt any equilibrium of the corrected surrogate game, the true planner welfare loss is at most \\\\(2\\\\sum_nh_n\\\\), true unilateral regret is at most \\\\(2h_n\\\\) for agent \\\\(n\\\\), and true utility is at least \\\\(-2h_n\\\\) relative to its normalized outside option. Payments balance exactly. The welfare and regret bounds compare two decisions, so their normalization constants cancel; they follow from the two-sided CVaR value error and surrogate optimality. Strong concavity also gives an allocation-distance bound \\\\(2\\\\sqrt{\\\\sum_nh_n/\\\\alpha}\\\\). These are static, conditional guarantees. \\\\ledger{17, 18, 22, 24}\\n\\n\\\\section{Objections and limits}\\n\\nThe payment correction resolves the original individual-rationality counterexample without changing strategic incentives. It does not restore Q's strict LMI or unique prices. P's convergence theorem concerns consensus on one shared decision and does not prove convergence of sampled play to Q's equilibrium set. Q's numerical sample rule, \\\\(Q_i=\\\\lceil0.96^{i+1}\\\\rceil\\\\), equals one at every iteration as written, whereas its convergence theorem requires geometrically increasing sample sizes. Q also guarantees capacity feasibility only at equilibrium. Feasible perturbation queries near nonnegative boundaries, multiplier existence, and an implementable learning rule remain conditions or open tasks. \\\\ledger{19, 20, 22}\\n\\n\\\\section{Outside sources and next step}\\n\\nA narrow prior-work search reported adjacent CVaR market work at \\\\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.06119} and stochastic aggregative-game learning at \\\\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.06023}. Neither source was used to prove the claims above, and the search does not establish novelty. \\\\ledger{16, 20}\\n\\nThe smallest decisive next step is to prove or refute convergence of a feasible, sample-based learning rule to the corrected game's equilibrium set, beginning with two agents and one resource. The present material establishes a conditional static connection; it is thin on dynamics and cannot support a publication-level novelty claim. \\\\ledger{20, 22, 24}\\n\\n\\\\end{document}\\n```\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'rg --files inputs ada emmy paper 2>/dev/null | sort'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat Q7P1.verdict.json && cat ledger.jsonl'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'rg --files inputs ada emmy paper 2>/dev/null | sort'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"ada/pair_note.md\\nemmy/transfer_audit.md\\ninputs/P.json\\ninputs/P.tex\\ninputs/Q.json\\ninputs/Q.tex\\npaper/paper.json\\npaper/pathfinder-meta.tex\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat Q7P1.verdict.json && cat ledger.jsonl'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"[\\n {\\n  \\\"round\\\": 1,\\n  \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:45:48Z\\\",\\n  \\\"note_sha256\\\": \\\"3edeaed6df3c5a63cbb0dffcb9328122abacb3abd308b8eca40aa80faa8a31a8\\\",\\n  \\\"decision\\\": \\\"ITERATE\\\",\\n  \\\"reason\\\": \\\"The connection is promising, but the note\\\\u2019s payment identity is wrong for general \\\\\\\\(N\\\\\\\\). Expanding Q\\\\u2019s Eq. (40) at equilibrium gives \\\\\\\\(t_n=(N+1)\\\\\\\\lambda^\\\\\\\\top(x_n-c/N)/(N-1)^2\\\\\\\\); the proposed rebate then gives \\\\\\\\((t_n+g_n)=\\\\\\\\lambda^\\\\\\\\top(x_n-c/N)/(N-1)^2\\\\\\\\), rather than the claimed coefficient \\\\\\\\(1\\\\\\\\). The ledger repeats the incorrect identity, so the transfer argument needs a fresh general-\\\\\\\\(N\\\\\\\\) check before the static result is drafted.\\\",\\n  \\\"action\\\": \\\"Recompute the equilibrium transfers for arbitrary \\\\\\\\(N\\\\\\\\), then verify budget balance and individual rationality using the corrected coefficient.\\\"\\n },\\n {\\n  \\\"round\\\": 2,\\n  \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:52:59Z\\\",\\n  \\\"note_sha256\\\": \\\"3edeaed6df3c5a63cbb0dffcb9328122abacb3abd308b8eca40aa80faa8a31a8\\\",\\n  \\\"decision\\\": \\\"DRAFT\\\",\\n  \\\"reason\\\": \\\"The ledger supports the note\\\\u2019s non-obvious static connection: an others-only transfer correction preserves Q\\\\u2019s efficient equilibrium allocations while restoring budget balance and individual rationality, and P\\\\u2019s empirical-CVaR bound yields quantitative guarantees for the true objective. The corrected transfer coefficient checks out for general \\\\\\\\(N\\\\\\\\). The note states the oracle and multiplier conditions and does not claim that P\\\\u2019s algorithm proves convergence for Q\\\\u2019s game.\\\",\\n  \\\"action\\\": null\\n }\\n]{\\\"seq\\\": 1, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:30:44Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"ada\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"intention\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"I am comparing Q\\\\u2019s LMI-designed balanced incentive game and mean-square best-response dynamics with P\\\\u2019s sampled CVaR zeroth-order optimization. I will first test whether P\\\\u2019s risk objective can be implemented by Q\\\\u2019s mechanism assumptions, then examine whether its sampling/communication method yields a genuinely new stability statement. I will cite exact paper lines and report obstructions.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 2, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:31:06Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"emmy\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"intention\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"I am testing whether Q\\\\u2019s quadratic tax and strong-monotonicity argument extend to risk-adjusted valuations \\\\\\\\(V_n(x_n)=-\\\\\\\\operatorname{CVaR}_{\\\\\\\\beta_n}(J_n(x_n,\\\\\\\\xi_n))\\\\\\\\), with P supplying a black-box empirical CVaR oracle. I will check Q\\\\u2019s exact curvature and sample-error requirements against P\\\\u2019s assumptions, and look for a smaller theorem or counterexample if a direct algorithm transplant fails.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 3, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:31:56Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"emmy\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"objection\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"A direct transplant of P\\\\u2019s Algorithm 1 into Q is invalid: P (Preliminaries and Eq. (update y)) has every agent maintain a copy of one common decision \\\\\\\\(x\\\\\\\\in\\\\\\\\mathcal X\\\\\\\\), and proves consensus; Q (manager problem (1)) optimizes heterogeneous allocations \\\\\\\\((x_1,\\\\\\\\ldots,x_N)\\\\\\\\) under \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\sum_n x_n\\\\\\\\le c\\\\\\\\). Consensus can destroy welfare: for \\\\\\\\(V_1(x)=2x-x^2/2\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(V_2(x)=x-x^2/2\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(c=1\\\\\\\\), the optimum is \\\\\\\\((1,0)\\\\\\\\), while equal allocations \\\\\\\\((1/2,1/2)\\\\\\\\) lose \\\\\\\\(1/4\\\\\\\\). P also assumes \\\\\\\\(r\\\\\\\\mathbb B\\\\\\\\subseteq\\\\\\\\mathcal X\\\\\\\\), incompatible with Q\\\\u2019s nonnegative allocation sets if copied literally. The reusable object is P\\\\u2019s empirical-CVaR oracle/error bound, not its consensus iteration.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 4, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:32:17Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"ada\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"idea\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"Sharpened question: can Q\\\\u2019s quadratic tax implement a risk-aware allocation when only P\\\\u2019s black-box sampled losses are available, with quantitative economic guarantees for the true CVaR game? Use P\\\\u2019s expected empirical CVaR plus ball smoothing as a fixed convex surrogate, add \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\gamma\\\\\\\\|x_n\\\\\\\\|^2/2\\\\\\\\) to ensure Q\\\\u2019s strong concavity, and normalize each valuation at \\\\\\\\(x_n=0\\\\\\\\). P\\\\u2019s bounded-loss DKW lemma (Appendix, Lemma cvar-estimation error bound) gives uniform-in-point \\\\\\\\(h_n=L_n\\\\\\\\delta_n+U_n\\\\\\\\sqrt{2\\\\\\\\pi}/(\\\\\\\\beta_n\\\\\\\\sqrt{s_n})\\\\\\\\) value error. Conditional on Q\\\\u2019s tax/LMI certificate for the surrogate, its unique equilibrium has true planner loss at most \\\\\\\\(2\\\\\\\\sum_n h_n\\\\\\\\), true unilateral regret at most \\\\\\\\(4h_n\\\\\\\\), exact payment balance, and true individual utility at least \\\\\\\\(-2h_n\\\\\\\\). This is a candidate theorem, not yet checked for the oracle-domain and Q proof issues. Emmy\\\\u2019s #3 correctly rules out importing P\\\\u2019s consensus iteration; I intend to use only its local CVaR estimator.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 5, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:33:12Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"ada\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"objection\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"Major correction to my #4: Q\\\\u2019s advertised LMI certificate is algebraically infeasible. Q Theorem nash_implementation_theorem, P2(i) (inputs/Q.tex around 240\\\\u2013250) requires \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\sum_m A_{nm}^n=0\\\\\\\\) for every \\\\\\\\(n\\\\\\\\). Q Proposition existence_uniqueness_prop, P1(ii) (around 200\\\\u2013213) requires \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\Psi+\\\\\\\\Psi^\\\\\\\\top\\\\\\\\preceq-\\\\\\\\upsilon I\\\\\\\\) with \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\upsilon>0\\\\\\\\), and its price-price block in row \\\\\\\\(n,m\\\\\\\\) is \\\\\\\\(-A_{nm}^n\\\\\\\\). Set a nonzero direction \\\\\\\\(v\\\\\\\\) with every allocation component zero and every price component the same \\\\\\\\(h\\\\\\\\). Then \\\\\\\\(v^\\\\\\\\top\\\\\\\\Psi v=-\\\\\\\\sum_n h^\\\\\\\\top(\\\\\\\\sum_m A_{nm}^n)h=0\\\\\\\\), hence \\\\\\\\(v^\\\\\\\\top(\\\\\\\\Psi+\\\\\\\\Psi^\\\\\\\\top)v=0\\\\\\\\), contradicting strict negativity. Thus no tax can satisfy Q\\\\u2019s stated P1 and P2 LMIs jointly, including Eq. (40); Q Proposition prop2 is false as stated. This does not by itself disprove equilibrium uniqueness via another argument. It blocks invoking Q\\\\u2019s strong-monotonicity certificate for the proposed CVaR hybrid. I am checking whether a narrower value-stability result survives without that certificate.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 6, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:33:25Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"emmy\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"objection\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"Critical check on Q: its proposed payment (Eq. (40)) cannot satisfy its own strict P1 LMI (Eq. (existence_uniqueness_cond_eq)), despite Proposition (prop2). For any \\\\\\\\(N\\\\\\\\ge2\\\\\\\\), its price-dependent own-price derivative is \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\partial_{p_n}U_n=-\\\\\\\\alpha[p_n-\\\\\\\\bar p_{-n}/(N-1)]+\\\\\\\\alpha(\\\\\\\\sum_m x_m-c)/(N-1)\\\\\\\\). Holding \\\\\\\\(x\\\\\\\\) fixed and moving all prices by the same vector \\\\\\\\(h\\\\\\\\) leaves this pseudogradient component unchanged. Thus the price-price Jacobian has common-price null vector \\\\\\\\((h,\\\\\\\\ldots,h)\\\\\\\\), so the full symmetric pseudogradient Jacobian has zero quadratic form on \\\\\\\\((\\\\\\\\Delta x=0,\\\\\\\\Delta p=(h,\\\\\\\\ldots,h))\\\\\\\\); it cannot be \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\preceq-\\\\\\\\upsilon I\\\\\\\\) for \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\upsilon>0\\\\\\\\). This blocks using Q\\\\u2019s claimed explicit LMI certificate as a theorem. It does not by itself prove nonunique NE; a different uniqueness proof or redesigned tax is needed. P\\\\u2019s CVaR oracle still provides a separate route to quantitative approximate-equilibrium guarantees.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 7, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:34:26Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"ada\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"finding\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"Beyond the LMI contradiction in #5/#6, Eq. (40) can have genuinely nonunique Nash equilibria. Take \\\\\\\\(N=2,K=1,c=2,\\\\\\\\mathcal X_1=\\\\\\\\mathcal X_2=[0,1],\\\\\\\\alpha=1\\\\\\\\), and \\\\\\\\(V_n(x)=2x-x^2/2\\\\\\\\). For each \\\\\\\\(p\\\\\\\\in[0,1]\\\\\\\\), the profile \\\\\\\\((x_1,p_1,x_2,p_2)=(1,p,1,p)\\\\\\\\) is NE. Directly from Q Eq. (40), \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\partial_{x_n}U_n=V_n\\\\\\\\prime(x_n)+p_n-2p_{-n}\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\partial_{p_n}U_n=-p_n+p_{-n}+x_1+x_2-c\\\\\\\\), and the own-strategy Hessian is \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\begin{psmallmatrix}-1&1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\1&-1\\\\\\\\end{psmallmatrix}\\\\\\\\preceq0\\\\\\\\). At the profile, these derivatives are \\\\\\\\(1-p\\\\\\\\ge0\\\\\\\\) at upper bound \\\\\\\\(x_n=1\\\\\\\\), and \\\\\\\\(0\\\\\\\\) for price, proving a global best response. Thus Q\\\\u2019s claimed unique NE fails under its stated compact, strongly concave valuation assumptions; optimum allocation remains unique. This is compatible with P\\\\u2019s genuinely stochastic risk model: let \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\xi=\\\\\\\\pm 1/4\\\\\\\\) equiprobably, \\\\\\\\(J_n(x,\\\\\\\\xi)=x^2/2-(9/4+\\\\\\\\xi)x\\\\\\\\), and take tail level \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\beta=1/2\\\\\\\\); then \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\operatorname{CVaR}_{1/2}J_n(x,\\\\\\\\xi)=x^2/2-2x\\\\\\\\) on \\\\\\\\([0,1]\\\\\\\\). P\\\\u2019s bounded convex Lipschitz sample-loss assumptions hold on this compact set. I will put the derivation in ada/.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 8, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:34:27Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"emmy\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"finding\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"Despite #5\\\\u20136, Q Eq. (40) appears to admit a direct full-implementation proof without strict monotonicity. For each resource \\\\\\\\(k\\\\\\\\), write \\\\\\\\(P^k=\\\\\\\\sum_n p_n^k\\\\\\\\) and \\\\\\\\(z^k=\\\\\\\\sum_n x_n^k-c^k\\\\\\\\). Q\\\\u2019s tax gives \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\partial_{p_n^k}U_n=-\\\\\\\\alpha(Np_n^k-P^k-z^k)/(N-1)\\\\\\\\). At a price best response, if any \\\\\\\\(p_n^k>0\\\\\\\\), then \\\\\\\\(Np_n^k=P^k+z^k>0\\\\\\\\), forcing every price positive/equal and then \\\\\\\\(z^k=0\\\\\\\\) by summation. If all prices vanish, the boundary derivative condition gives \\\\\\\\(z^k\\\\\\\\le0\\\\\\\\). Hence every NE has common \\\\\\\\(p^k\\\\\\\\ge0\\\\\\\\), feasible allocations, and \\\\\\\\(p^k z^k=0\\\\\\\\). At common prices, \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\partial_{x_n}t_n=\\\\\\\\alpha p\\\\\\\\), so each allocation best response maximizes \\\\\\\\(V_n(x_n)-\\\\\\\\alpha p^\\\\\\\\top x_n\\\\\\\\). These are precisely planner KKT conditions with \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\lambda=\\\\\\\\alpha p\\\\\\\\), proving every NE allocation optimal for concave \\\\\\\\(V_n\\\\\\\\), even nonsmooth via subgradients. Conversely a planner primal-dual pair yields an NE (assuming a multiplier exists). Q\\\\u2019s unique-message claim fails: \\\\\\\\(N=2,K=1,\\\\\\\\mathcal X_n=[0,1],c=2,V_n(x)=2x-x^2/2,\\\\\\\\alpha=1\\\\\\\\) has \\\\\\\\(x_1=x_2=1\\\\\\\\) and any common \\\\\\\\(p\\\\\\\\in[0,1]\\\\\\\\) as NE. This outcome-only theorem is the safer foundation for combining Q with P\\\\u2019s CVaR oracle.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 9, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:35:09Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"emmy\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"correction\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"Qualification to #8: the converse from planner primal-dual pair to a Nash equilibrium needs Q\\\\u2019s \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\alpha\\\\\\\\)-strong concavity (or another condition making each player\\\\u2019s objective jointly concave in \\\\\\\\((x_n,p_n)\\\\\\\\)), not mere concavity. In Eq. (40), the own \\\\\\\\((x_n,p_n)\\\\\\\\) cross coefficient is \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\alpha/(N-1)\\\\\\\\), while the own price curvature is \\\\\\\\(-\\\\\\\\alpha\\\\\\\\). Combining Q\\\\u2019s \\\\\\\\(-\\\\\\\\alpha\\\\\\\\) curvature in \\\\\\\\(x_n\\\\\\\\) gives a negative semidefinite own Hessian for \\\\\\\\(N\\\\\\\\ge2\\\\\\\\); then the first-order conditions suffice. The forward statement, every existing NE allocates optimally, only needs concavity of the planner valuation and the price best-response identities.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 10, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:36:09Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"ada\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"finding\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"Further correction: Q Eq. (40) also fails its individual-rationality claim under stated assumptions. Let \\\\\\\\(N=2,K=1,c=1,\\\\\\\\mathcal X_n=[0,1],\\\\\\\\alpha=1\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(V_1(x)=4x-x^2/2\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(V_2(x)=x-x^2/2\\\\\\\\). The social optimum is \\\\\\\\((x_1,x_2)=(1,0)\\\\\\\\). At common price \\\\\\\\(p_1=p_2=3\\\\\\\\), all price derivatives vanish; \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\partial_{x_1}U_1=V_1\\\\\\\\prime(1)-3=0\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\partial_{x_2}U_2=V_2\\\\\\\\prime(0)-3=-2\\\\\\\\le0\\\\\\\\), and own utilities are jointly concave, so this is an NE. Substitution into Q Eq. (40) gives \\\\\\\\(t_1=3p(x_1-c/2)=9/2\\\\\\\\), whereas \\\\\\\\(V_1(1)=7/2\\\\\\\\); hence \\\\\\\\(U_1=-1<0=V_1(0)\\\\\\\\). The IR proof\\\\u2019s proposed zero-demand deviation also fails: holding prices and rival fixed, \\\\\\\\(t_1((0,3),(0,3))=3/2>0\\\\\\\\). This can arise from P-compatible CVaR losses \\\\\\\\(J_n(x,\\\\\\\\xi)=x^2/2-(b_n+1/4+\\\\\\\\xi)x\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(b_1=4,b_2=1\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\xi=\\\\\\\\pm1/4\\\\\\\\) equiprobably, tail \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\beta=1/2\\\\\\\\). Thus my #4 candidate theorem must drop exact/approximate IR for Eq. (40), unless a redesigned transfer rule is proved. Exact budget balance and optimal allocation still survive. I will document this counterexample in ada/.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 11, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:36:20Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"emmy\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"objection\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"Further failure of Q Eq. (40): individual rationality can fail at a genuine NE. Let \\\\\\\\(N=2,K=1,\\\\\\\\mathcal X_1=\\\\\\\\mathcal X_2=[0,1],c=1,\\\\\\\\alpha=0.1\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(V_1(x)=1.1x-0.05x^2\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(V_2(x)=0.5x-0.05x^2\\\\\\\\). The planner optimum is \\\\\\\\((1,0)\\\\\\\\), with multiplier \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\lambda=1\\\\\\\\); Eq. (40) has an NE at \\\\\\\\((x_1,x_2,p_1,p_2)=(1,0,10,10)\\\\\\\\). Indeed the own payoff Hessian is \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\begin{psmallmatrix}-0.1&0.1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\0.1&-0.1\\\\\\\\end{psmallmatrix}\\\\\\\\preceq0\\\\\\\\), and at this profile price derivatives vanish, agent 1\\\\u2019s allocation derivative is \\\\\\\\(V_1\\\\\\\\prime(1)-1=0\\\\\\\\), agent 2\\\\u2019s is \\\\\\\\(V_2\\\\\\\\prime(0)-1=-0.5\\\\\\\\) at lower bound. Direct substitution in Eq. (40) gives \\\\\\\\(t_1=1.5\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(t_2=-1.5\\\\\\\\). Thus \\\\\\\\(U_1=V_1(1)-t_1=1.05-1.5=-0.45<0=V_1(0)\\\\\\\\), contradicting Q\\\\u2019s P4/Proposition (prop2). P-compatible stochastic losses realizing these valuations are \\\\\\\\(J_1(x,\\\\\\\\xi)=0.05x^2-(1.2+\\\\\\\\xi)x\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(J_2(x,\\\\\\\\xi)=0.05x^2-(0.6+\\\\\\\\xi)x\\\\\\\\), with \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\xi=\\\\\\\\pm0.1\\\\\\\\) equiprobably and CVaR upper-tail level \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\beta=1/2\\\\\\\\); then \\\\\\\\(-\\\\\\\\operatorname{CVaR}_{1/2}J_n=V_n\\\\\\\\) on \\\\\\\\([0,1]\\\\\\\\), and each sample loss is convex, bounded, Lipschitz. A Q\\\\u2013P synthesis should target efficient allocations and budget balance first, then redesign transfers if exact IR is required.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 12, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:36:58Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"ada\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"idea\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"Proposed exact repair of Q Eq. (40) IR, without changing any best response: write \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\kappa_N=(N^2-N+1)/(N-1)^2=1+N/(N-1)^2\\\\\\\\). Direct substitution at any common-price complementary NE gives \\\\\\\\(t_n=\\\\\\\\alpha\\\\\\\\kappa_N p^\\\\\\\\top(x_n-c/N)\\\\\\\\) (terms involving \\\\\\\\(p^\\\\\\\\top(\\\\\\\\sum_mx_m-c)\\\\\\\\) vanish). Add the others-only rebate \\\\\\\\(R_n(s_{-n})=\\\\\\\\alpha(\\\\\\\\kappa_N-1)(\\\\\\\\bar p_{-n}/(N-1))^\\\\\\\\top(\\\\\\\\bar x_{-n}-(N-1)c/N)\\\\\\\\), and define \\\\\\\\(t_n^\\\\\\\\mathrm{new}=t_n+R_n\\\\\\\\). At NE, \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\bar p_{-n}/(N-1)=p\\\\\\\\) and \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\bar x_{-n}=c-x_n\\\\\\\\) in every priced coordinate, so \\\\\\\\(t_n^\\\\\\\\mathrm{new}=\\\\\\\\alpha p^\\\\\\\\top(x_n-c/N)\\\\\\\\). Hence \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\sum_n t_n^\\\\\\\\mathrm{new}=0\\\\\\\\) and \\\\\\\\(U_n^\\\\\\\\mathrm{new}\\\\\\\\ge V_n(0)+\\\\\\\\alpha p^\\\\\\\\top c/N\\\\\\\\ge0\\\\\\\\), since \\\\\\\\(x_n\\\\\\\\) maximizes \\\\\\\\(V_n(x_n)-\\\\\\\\alpha p^\\\\\\\\top x_n\\\\\\\\) and \\\\\\\\(0\\\\\\\\in\\\\\\\\mathcal X_n\\\\\\\\). The rebate uses no \\\\\\\\(s_n\\\\\\\\), so NE set and efficient allocation are unchanged. Need check the formula carefully for slack coordinates and transfer signs; this could be the constructive Q\\\\u2013P line once paired with P\\\\u2019s CVaR surrogate oracle.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 13, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:37:05Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"emmy\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"idea\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"Transfer repair for Q Eq. (40), independent of valuation reports: at any NE, price complementarity from #8 gives \\\\\\\\(p_n=p\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\lambda=\\\\\\\\alpha p\\\\\\\\), and \\\\\\\\(p^\\\\\\\\top(\\\\\\\\sum_mx_m-c)=0\\\\\\\\). Direct simplification of Eq. (40) gives \\\\\\\\(t_n=A_N\\\\\\\\lambda^\\\\\\\\top(x_n-c/N)\\\\\\\\), with \\\\\\\\(A_N=(N^2-N+1)/(N-1)^2=1+N/(N-1)^2\\\\\\\\). Define an added payment depending only on others\\\\u2019 messages, \\\\\\\\(g_n(s_{-n})=\\\\\\\\alpha N/(N-1)^3\\\\\\\\,\\\\\\\\bar p_{-n}^\\\\\\\\top[\\\\\\\\bar x_{-n}-(N-1)c/N]\\\\\\\\), and \\\\\\\\(t_n^{\\\\\\\\rm new}=t_n+g_n\\\\\\\\). Since \\\\\\\\(g_n\\\\\\\\) is independent of \\\\\\\\(s_n\\\\\\\\), the entire NE set and all best responses are unchanged. At every NE, complementarity gives \\\\\\\\(g_n=(1-A_N)\\\\\\\\lambda^\\\\\\\\top(x_n-c/N)\\\\\\\\), hence \\\\\\\\(t_n^{\\\\\\\\rm new}=\\\\\\\\lambda^\\\\\\\\top(x_n-c/N)\\\\\\\\). Thus \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\sum_n t_n^{\\\\\\\\rm new}=\\\\\\\\lambda^\\\\\\\\top(\\\\\\\\sum_nx_n-c)=0\\\\\\\\), and \\\\\\\\(U_n^{\\\\\\\\rm new}=V_n(x_n)-\\\\\\\\lambda^\\\\\\\\top x_n+\\\\\\\\lambda^\\\\\\\\top c/N\\\\\\\\ge V_n(0)+\\\\\\\\lambda^\\\\\\\\top c/N\\\\\\\\ge V_n(0)\\\\\\\\), because each \\\\\\\\(x_n\\\\\\\\) maximizes \\\\\\\\(V_n(x_n)-\\\\\\\\lambda^\\\\\\\\top x_n\\\\\\\\) and \\\\\\\\(0\\\\\\\\in\\\\\\\\mathcal X_n\\\\\\\\). This appears to restore exact IR and BB without changing Q\\\\u2019s efficient equilibrium allocations; I am checking algebra and edge cases.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 14, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:37:56Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"emmy\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"finding\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"Sharper structural theorem for Q Eq. (40) and the repaired tax in #12\\\\u201313: assume \\\\\\\\(N\\\\\\\\ge2\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(0\\\\\\\\in\\\\\\\\mathcal X_n\\\\\\\\) compact convex, \\\\\\\\(V_n\\\\\\\\) upper semicontinuous and \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\alpha\\\\\\\\)-strongly concave, and existence of a planner multiplier. Then the NE set is exactly \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\{(x^\\\\\\\\star,p_1=\\\\\\\\cdots=p_N=\\\\\\\\lambda/\\\\\\\\alpha):\\\\\\\\lambda\\\\\\\\in\\\\\\\\Lambda^\\\\\\\\star\\\\\\\\}\\\\\\\\), where \\\\\\\\(x^\\\\\\\\star\\\\\\\\) is the unique welfare-maximizing allocation and \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\Lambda^\\\\\\\\star\\\\\\\\) is the (possibly non-singleton) optimal multiplier set. Necessity follows from price best responses and allocation best responses (#8). Sufficiency follows because each player\\\\u2019s payoff is jointly concave in \\\\\\\\((x_n,p_n)\\\\\\\\): Q\\\\u2019s strong curvature gives own Hessian bounded by \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\alpha\\\\\\\\begin{psmallmatrix}-I&I/(N-1)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\I/(N-1)&-I\\\\\\\\end{psmallmatrix}\\\\\\\\preceq0\\\\\\\\), so KKT conditions imply a global best response. The others-only repair preserves this set and proves BB+IR for every equilibrium, including nonunique prices. Thus the correct dynamic target is the equilibrium set or unique allocation, not a unique message profile.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 15, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:38:13Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"emmy\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"finding\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"Quantitative Q\\\\u2013P synthesis conditional on solving the learning step: for each agent let \\\\\\\\(C_n(x)=\\\\\\\\operatorname{CVaR}_{\\\\\\\\beta_n}J_n(x,\\\\\\\\xi_n)\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(H_{n,s}(x)=\\\\\\\\mathbb E[\\\\\\\\widehat C_{n,s}(x)]\\\\\\\\) for a fresh size-\\\\\\\\(s_n\\\\\\\\) empirical CVaR batch, and normalized valuations \\\\\\\\(V_n(x)=u_n(x)-C_n(x)-[u_n(0)-C_n(0)]\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\widetilde V_n(x)=u_n(x)-H_{n,s}(x)-[u_n(0)-H_{n,s}(0)]\\\\\\\\). If P\\\\u2019s sample losses are convex, \\\\\\\\(L_n\\\\\\\\)-Lipschitz and bounded by \\\\\\\\(U_n\\\\\\\\), and \\\\\\\\(u_n\\\\\\\\) is \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\alpha\\\\\\\\)-strongly concave, both valuations are \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\alpha\\\\\\\\)-strongly concave. P Appendix CVaR-stability lemma plus its DKW integration gives \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\sup_x|H_{n,s}(x)-C_n(x)|\\\\\\\\le\\\\\\\\kappa_n:=U_n\\\\\\\\sqrt{2\\\\\\\\pi}/(\\\\\\\\beta_n\\\\\\\\sqrt{s_n})\\\\\\\\); this is a deterministic bound on the expected surrogate, with no uniform-over-samples claim. Thus \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\sup_x|V_n-\\\\\\\\widetilde V_n|\\\\\\\\le\\\\\\\\varepsilon_n:=2\\\\\\\\kappa_n\\\\\\\\). Run Q Eq. (40) with the others-only transfer repair #13 for the surrogate game. At any surrogate NE \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\widetilde s\\\\\\\\), the true planner welfare gap is at most \\\\\\\\(2\\\\\\\\sum_n\\\\\\\\varepsilon_n=4\\\\\\\\sum_n\\\\\\\\kappa_n\\\\\\\\); true unilateral gain from any deviation is at most \\\\\\\\(2\\\\\\\\varepsilon_n=4\\\\\\\\kappa_n\\\\\\\\); total payments are exactly zero; and true utility is at least \\\\\\\\(-\\\\\\\\varepsilon_n=-2\\\\\\\\kappa_n\\\\\\\\) relative to the normalized outside option. Strong concavity further gives \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\|\\\\\\\\widetilde x-x^\\\\\\\\star\\\\\\\\|\\\\\\\\le2\\\\\\\\sqrt{\\\\\\\\sum_n\\\\\\\\varepsilon_n/\\\\\\\\alpha}\\\\\\\\). These are direct two-sided value perturbation bounds plus #14, not consequences of P\\\\u2019s consensus theorem. If \\\\\\\\(J_n(0,\\\\\\\\xi)=0\\\\\\\\) a.s., normalization adds no extra error and every factor \\\\\\\\(2\\\\\\\\) from \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\varepsilon_n=2\\\\\\\\kappa_n\\\\\\\\) drops.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 16, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:38:44Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"emmy\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"finding\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"Narrow prior-work check (not a novelty claim): searched `site:arxiv.org risk averse CVaR mechanism design budget balance Nash implementation resource allocation`, `site:arxiv.org conditional value at risk mechanism design distributed resource allocation Nash equilibrium budget balanced`, `site:arxiv.org zeroth order CVaR generalized Nash equilibrium stochastic aggregative game`, `\\\\\\\"CVaR\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"budget balanced\\\\\\\" mechanism design resource allocation`, and `\\\\\\\"CVaR\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"Nash implementation\\\\\\\" mechanism design`. Relevant adjacent papers found: Dahlin\\\\u2013Jain, https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.06119, includes CVaR in a two-stage market but explicitly assumes nonstrategic agents for its market mechanism (abstract); Du\\\\u2013Meng, https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.06023, gives distributed learning for stochastic aggregative games under expectation-valued costs and strong monotonicity (abstract), assumptions unavailable for Q\\\\u2019s Eq. (40) because #5\\\\u20136. These do not establish novelty of our repaired-tax plus CVaR-oracle direction; a fuller literature review remains.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 17, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:39:04Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"ada\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"correction\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"My #4 overstated the true-game regret and assumed IR from the original tax. After the others-only repair #12\\\\u201313, the bounds sharpen because valuation normalization constants cancel in comparisons: if \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\sup_x|\\\\\\\\widetilde C_n(x)-C_n(x)|\\\\\\\\le h_n\\\\\\\\), then a surrogate NE has true unilateral regret \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\le2h_n\\\\\\\\) and true planner loss \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\le2\\\\\\\\sum_nh_n\\\\\\\\), although \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\sup_x|\\\\\\\\widetilde V_n-V_n|\\\\\\\\le2h_n\\\\\\\\). True individual utility is \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\ge-2h_n\\\\\\\\), and transfers balance exactly. With P\\\\u2019s fixed-sample expected empirical CVaR plus smoothing, \\\\\\\\(h_n=L_n\\\\\\\\delta_n+U_n\\\\\\\\sqrt{2\\\\\\\\pi}/(\\\\\\\\beta_n\\\\\\\\sqrt{s_n})\\\\\\\\) under oracle access on a \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\delta_n\\\\\\\\)-neighborhood. The precise derivation, Q counterexamples, tax repair, and remaining dynamic gap are in ada/pair_note.md; both style checks pass. This refines #15\\\\u2019s generic factors by exploiting cancellation, and includes smoothing needed for Q-style gradient play.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 18, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:39:20Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"emmy\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"correction\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"Sharpening #15 after cross-checking ada/pair_note.md: the normalization constants cancel when comparing two allocations or a unilateral deviation. Let \\\\\\\\(d_n(x)=H_{n,s}(x)-C_n(x)\\\\\\\\), with \\\\\\\\(|d_n(x)|\\\\\\\\le\\\\\\\\kappa_n\\\\\\\\). Then \\\\\\\\((V_n-\\\\\\\\widetilde V_n)(x)=d_n(x)-d_n(0)\\\\\\\\), so differences between two decisions have magnitude at most \\\\\\\\(2\\\\\\\\kappa_n\\\\\\\\), not \\\\\\\\(4\\\\\\\\kappa_n\\\\\\\\). Consequently the true welfare gap at a surrogate NE is \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\le2\\\\\\\\sum_n\\\\\\\\kappa_n\\\\\\\\), true unilateral regret is \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\le2\\\\\\\\kappa_n\\\\\\\\), and strong concavity gives \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\|\\\\\\\\widetilde x-x^\\\\\\\\star\\\\\\\\|\\\\\\\\le2\\\\\\\\sqrt{\\\\\\\\sum_n\\\\\\\\kappa_n/\\\\\\\\alpha}\\\\\\\\). The true IR shortfall remains \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\le2\\\\\\\\kappa_n\\\\\\\\), since it compares normalized utility with zero. Ada\\\\u2019s note has these sharper factors; #15 remains valid but loose. The same cancellation holds with P\\\\u2019s smoothing term \\\\\\\\(h_n=L_n\\\\\\\\delta_n+\\\\\\\\kappa_n\\\\\\\\) wherever its queries are feasible.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 19, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:39:51Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"ada\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"objection\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"An additional dynamic-evidence mismatch in Q: Theorem theorem4 (inputs/Q.tex around lines 518\\\\u2013523) assumes \\\\\\\\(Q_i=\\\\\\\\lceil C_b/\\\\\\\\eta^{2(i+1)}\\\\\\\\rceil\\\\\\\\) with \\\\\\\\(0<\\\\\\\\eta<1\\\\\\\\), so sample size grows geometrically. Its numerical section (around lines 678\\\\u2013685) instead says \\\\\\\\(Q_i=\\\\\\\\lceil\\\\\\\\widetilde\\\\\\\\eta^{i+1}\\\\\\\\rceil\\\\\\\\) with \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\widetilde\\\\\\\\eta=0.96\\\\\\\\), which equals \\\\\\\\(1\\\\\\\\) at every iteration. Thus the plotted trajectories do not instantiate the theorem\\\\u2019s sampling regime as written. This reinforces that a Q\\\\u2013P paper should prove its own learning statement; P\\\\u2019s fixed-batch result targets a shared decision and cannot certify Q\\\\u2019s game.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 20, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:39:54Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"emmy\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"finding\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"Scope and remaining gap for a publication: I sharpened the pair question from \\\\u201cis there a connection?\\\\u201d to \\\\u201ccan an efficient, balanced, individually rational resource-allocation mechanism be learned from black-box CVaR losses with a finite-sample economic guarantee?\\\\u201d The static answer is the repaired-tax theorem (#14, #18; also ada/pair_note.md). P\\\\u2019s objective is the sum/average of individual CVaRs (P Preliminaries and Appendix remark), so this does not optimize \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\operatorname{CVaR}(\\\\\\\\sum_nJ_n)\\\\\\\\) in general. A full paper still needs a decentralized zeroth-order or primal-dual learning theorem for Q\\\\u2019s heterogeneous allocations, with feasible perturbation queries near nonnegative boundaries, and convergence to the equilibrium set despite nonunique prices and lack of Q\\\\u2019s strict monotonicity. P\\\\u2019s exact-consensus theorem solves a different shared-decision problem and cannot supply that step. Fixed fresh-batch expected empirical CVaR is a deterministic surrogate; its value bound alone does not prove that an implementable strategic learning rule reaches its equilibrium. Q also allows interim capacity violations because its allocation rule is \\\\\\\\(y_n=x_n\\\\\\\\) and feasibility emerges only at equilibrium. The prior-work search in #16 was narrow, so novelty is unresolved.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 21, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:40:08Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"emmy\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"ready\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"ready at #20\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": 20}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 22, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:40:46Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"ada\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"finding\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"Final independent account is in ada/pair_note.md (style-gates and local artifact ban both pass). Q Eq. (40) has efficient equilibrium allocations but its claimed strict P1 LMI is incompatible with P2(i); prices need not be unique, and original transfers can violate IR (#5, #7, #10). The others-only rebate in #12 restores exact equilibrium budget balance and IR without changing best responses; I checked the general coefficient algebra for several \\\\\\\\(N\\\\\\\\) and the explicit IR counterexample numerically. Pairing that repaired tax with P\\\\u2019s fixed fresh-batch expected empirical CVaR plus smoothing yields a deterministic surrogate with \\\\\\\\(h_n=L_n\\\\\\\\delta_n+U_n\\\\\\\\sqrt{2\\\\\\\\pi}/(\\\\\\\\beta_n\\\\\\\\sqrt{s_n})\\\\\\\\) pointwise value error, hence true welfare loss \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\le2\\\\\\\\sum_nh_n\\\\\\\\), true unilateral regret \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\le2h_n\\\\\\\\), and true utility \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\ge-2h_n\\\\\\\\) at any surrogate NE, conditional on an oracle neighborhood and a planner multiplier. Exact dynamic convergence from P\\\\u2019s samples remains unproved: P\\\\u2019s consensus theorem addresses a shared decision, whereas Q has separate coupled allocations and nonunique prices. Emmy\\\\u2019s #20 agrees on this boundary. No claim of publication novelty follows from this pair alone.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 23, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:40:52Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"ada\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"ready\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"ready at #22\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": 22}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 24, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:42:01Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"emmy\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"finding\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"Reopened after ada #22 and independently checked its core algebra against Q Eq. (40), lines 412\\\\u2013422, and P Appendix CVaR/DKW bound, lines 1287\\\\u20131315. At a common-price complementary equilibrium, Q\\\\u2019s original tax is \\\\\\\\(t_n=\\\\\\\\alpha[1+N/(N-1)^2]p^\\\\\\\\top(x_n-c/N)\\\\\\\\). The others-only rebate in #13 therefore leaves every best response intact and reduces the equilibrium tax to \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\alpha p^\\\\\\\\top(x_n-c/N)\\\\\\\\), giving exact balance and participation when \\\\\\\\(0\\\\\\\\in\\\\\\\\mathcal X_n\\\\\\\\) and valuations are normalized. P\\\\u2019s \\\\\\\\(2U/\\\\\\\\beta\\\\\\\\) CVaR CDF bound and integrated DKW bound yield \\\\\\\\(U\\\\\\\\sqrt{2\\\\\\\\pi}/(\\\\\\\\beta\\\\\\\\sqrt{s})\\\\\\\\) in expected absolute value at each point; because this constant is point-independent, the expected empirical surrogate obeys the deterministic bound used by ada. The sharper \\\\\\\\(2h_n\\\\\\\\) regret and \\\\\\\\(2\\\\\\\\sum_nh_n\\\\\\\\) welfare bounds in #17/#22 follow by canceling valuation-normalization constants. I found no new obstruction to the static theorem. Dynamic convergence, feasible perturbation queries, multiplier existence, and literature novelty remain unresolved; P\\\\u2019s common-decision consensus theorem does not discharge them.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 25, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:42:11Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"emmy\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"ready\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"ready at #24\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": 24}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 26, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:42:54Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"ada\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"finding\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"Final source cross-check: Q Eq. (40), inputs/Q.tex lines 410\\\\u2013422, gives the price derivative and common-price equilibrium tax in ada/pair_note.md; Q P1(ii) and P2(i), lines 200\\\\u2013250, still contradict along a common-price direction. P Assumptions Lipschitz/convex, inputs/P.tex lines 495\\\\u2013511, and its Appendix CVaR/DKW estimate, lines 1287\\\\u20131315, support the expected empirical surrogate and pointwise error \\\\\\\\(h_n=L_n\\\\\\\\delta_n+U_n\\\\\\\\sqrt{2\\\\\\\\pi}/(\\\\\\\\beta_n\\\\\\\\sqrt{s_n})\\\\\\\\), provided queries are possible on the full perturbation neighborhood. The rebate is independent of agent \\\\\\\\(n\\\\\\\\) messages, so the Q equilibrium set stays fixed; it yields true welfare gap \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\le 2\\\\\\\\sum_n h_n\\\\\\\\) and unilateral regret \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\le 2h_n\\\\\\\\) by cancellation of normalization constants. I found no new obstruction to this static theorem. Implementable convergence, feasible perturbation queries, multiplier existence, and novelty remain unresolved; P Algorithm 1 uses consensus over one common decision, not Q heterogeneous coupled allocations.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 27, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:43:00Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"ada\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"ready\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"ready at #26\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": 26}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 28, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:45:48Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"verifier\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"review\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"ITERATE: The connection is promising, but the note\\\\u2019s payment identity is wrong for general \\\\\\\\(N\\\\\\\\). Expanding Q\\\\u2019s Eq. (40) at equilibrium gives \\\\\\\\(t_n=(N+1)\\\\\\\\lambda^\\\\\\\\top(x_n-c/N)/(N-1)^2\\\\\\\\); the proposed rebate then gives \\\\\\\\((t_n+g_n)=\\\\\\\\lambda^\\\\\\\\top(x_n-c/N)/(N-1)^2\\\\\\\\), rather than the claimed coefficient \\\\\\\\(1\\\\\\\\). The ledger repeats the incorrect identity, so the transfer argument needs a fresh general-\\\\\\\\(N\\\\\\\\) check before the static result is drafted. Action: Recompute the equilibrium transfers for arbitrary \\\\\\\\(N\\\\\\\\), then verify budget balance and individual rationality using the corrected coefficient.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 29, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:46:50Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"emmy\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"correction\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"Verifier #28 is incorrect for Q Eq. (40), lines 412\\\\u2013422: it omits the third term\\\\u2019s factor \\\\\\\\(N/(N-1)\\\\\\\\). At common price \\\\\\\\(p_m=p\\\\\\\\), write \\\\\\\\(z=\\\\\\\\sum_mx_m-c\\\\\\\\) and \\\\\\\\(d_n=x_n-c/N\\\\\\\\). The price-quadratic terms cancel, the third term is \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\alpha Np^\\\\\\\\top d_n/(N-1)\\\\\\\\), and the final term is \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\alpha[p^\\\\\\\\top d_n-p^\\\\\\\\top z]/(N-1)^2\\\\\\\\). Thus \\\\\\\\(t_n/\\\\\\\\alpha=[N/(N-1)+1/(N-1)^2]p^\\\\\\\\top d_n-[1/(N-1)+1/(N-1)^2]p^\\\\\\\\top z\\\\\\\\). At complementary equilibrium \\\\\\\\(p^\\\\\\\\top z=0\\\\\\\\), the coefficient is \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\kappa_N=(N^2-N+1)/(N-1)^2\\\\\\\\), as in #12\\\\u201313 and ada/pair_note.md. The rebate in #13 contributes \\\\\\\\(-\\\\\\\\alpha Np^\\\\\\\\top d_n/(N-1)^2\\\\\\\\), leaving \\\\\\\\(t_n^{\\\\\\\\rm new}=\\\\\\\\alpha p^\\\\\\\\top d_n\\\\\\\\), so balance and IR remain valid. For \\\\\\\\(N=3,c=3,x=(2,1,0),p=1,n=1,\\\\\\\\alpha=1\\\\\\\\), Eq. (40) gives \\\\\\\\(t_1=7/4\\\\\\\\), rebate \\\\\\\\(-3/4\\\\\\\\), and revised transfer \\\\\\\\(1\\\\\\\\); #28 predicts \\\\\\\\(1\\\\\\\\) for the original tax and is contradicted directly.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 30, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:48:22Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"emmy\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"finding\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"Full general-\\\\\\\\(N\\\\\\\\) audit is in emmy/transfer_audit.md (style gate and artifact ban pass). It expands all five terms of Q Eq. (40), derives \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\kappa_N=(N^2-N+1)/(N-1)^2\\\\\\\\), and checks the rebate leaves \\\\\\\\(t_n^{\\\\\\\\rm new}=\\\\\\\\alpha p^\\\\\\\\top(x_n-c/N)\\\\\\\\), exact equilibrium budget balance, and IR. The \\\\\\\\(N=3\\\\\\\\) countercheck to verifier #28 is a genuine NE under Q assumptions: \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\mathcal X_n=[0,2]\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(c=3\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\alpha=1\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(V_1(x)=3x-x^2/2\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(V_2(x)=2x-x^2/2\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(V_3(x)=x/2-x^2/2\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(x=(2,1,0)\\\\\\\\), and all \\\\\\\\(p_n=1\\\\\\\\). Own payoff gradients satisfy KKT and the Hessian is negative definite; Q Eq. (40) gives \\\\\\\\(t_1=7/4\\\\\\\\), rebate \\\\\\\\(-3/4\\\\\\\\), revised tax \\\\\\\\(1\\\\\\\\). The coefficient in #28 instead predicts \\\\\\\\(t_1=1\\\\\\\\). Static Q\\\\u2013P conclusions in #17/#22 survive this audit; implementable learning and novelty remain unresolved.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 31, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:48:37Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"emmy\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"ready\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"ready at #30\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": 30}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 32, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:48:38Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"ada\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"finding\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"Independent audit of verifier #28 agrees with emmy #29\\\\u201330 and resolves the coefficient objection. From Q Eq. (40), at common price \\\\\\\\(p\\\\\\\\), set \\\\\\\\(d_n=x_n-c/N\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(z=\\\\\\\\sum_mx_m-c\\\\\\\\). The first and fourth terms cancel; the third contributes \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\alpha Np^\\\\\\\\top d_n/(N-1)\\\\\\\\), and the last contributes \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\alpha p^\\\\\\\\top(d_n-z)/(N-1)^2\\\\\\\\). With the second term, \\\\\\\\(t_n/\\\\\\\\alpha=\\\\\\\\kappa_Np^\\\\\\\\top d_n-Np^\\\\\\\\top z/(N-1)^2\\\\\\\\), where \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\kappa_N=(N^2-N+1)/(N-1)^2\\\\\\\\). Our others-only rebate is \\\\\\\\(R_n/\\\\\\\\alpha=Np^\\\\\\\\top(z-d_n)/(N-1)^2\\\\\\\\) at common prices, so \\\\\\\\(t_n^{\\\\\\\\mathrm{new}}=\\\\\\\\alpha p^\\\\\\\\top d_n\\\\\\\\) for any allocation profile with common prices, even before complementarity. I checked the literal five-term tax with exact rational arithmetic for \\\\\\\\(N=2,\\\\\\\\ldots,8\\\\\\\\), including nonzero \\\\\\\\(z\\\\\\\\). The full expansion is now in ada/pair_note.md; both Markdown style gates pass. At equilibrium, Q price best responses give \\\\\\\\(p^\\\\\\\\top z=0\\\\\\\\), and the earlier balance and IR proof follows. The Q\\\\u2013P static CVaR surrogate bound survives; implementable learning, oracle geometry, multiplier existence, and novelty remain unresolved.\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": null}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 33, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:48:44Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"ada\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"ready\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"ready at #32\\\", \\\"supersedes\\\": null, \\\"seen\\\": 32}\\n{\\\"seq\\\": 34, \\\"at\\\": \\\"2026-09-25T17:49:53Z\\\", \\\"actor\\\": \\\"emmy\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"finding\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"Independent check of ada #32 against Q Eq. (40), inputs/Q.tex lines 412\\\\u2013422: with common price \\\\\\\\(p\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(d_n=x_n-c/N\\\\\\\\), and \\\\\\\\(z=\\\\\\\\sum_mx_m-c\\\\\\\\), the literal tax gives \\\\\\\\(t_n/\\\\\\\\alpha=\\\\\\\\kappa_N p^\\\\\\\\top d_n-Np^\\\\\\\\top z/(N-1)^2\\\\\\\\), where \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\kappa_N=1+N/(N-1)^2\\\\\\\\). The others-only rebate from #13 gives \\\\\\\\(g_n/\\\\\\\\alpha=Np^\\\\\\