de\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_32\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"pdfinfo paper/paper.pdf | rg 'Pages:|Page size:|File size:'; pdftotext -layout paper/paper.pdf - | sed -n '1,90p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_32\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"pdfinfo paper/paper.pdf | rg 'Pages:|Page size:|File size:'; pdftotext -layout paper/paper.pdf - | sed -n '1,90p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"Pages:           4\\nPage size:       595.276 x 841.89 pts (A4)\\nFile size:       323456 bytes\\n  PATHFINDER PAPER \u00b7 Q7P1          \u00b7   2026-09-25\\n\\n\\n\\n  Balanced risk-aware allocation from an\\n  empirical CVaR oracle\\n  Research phase: DRAFT after 2 rounds, 1 ITERATE, 0 REVISE. Edit phase: paper, review round 1\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n  Abstract\\n\\n  We examine the resource-allocation payment of Garjani, Shokri and Kebriaei together with the sampled\\n  conditional value at risk (CVaR) estimator of Wang, Huang, Xu and Johansson. The original payment\\n  implements an optimal allocation, but its stated strict uniqueness certificate is incompatible with its\\n  implementation condition, prices can be non-unique, and individual rationality can fail. An explicit\\n  transfer depending only on other agents\u2019 messages preserves the equilibrium set and gives exact budget\\n  balance and individual rationality at every equilibrium. For a fixed expected empirical-CVaR surrogate,\\n                                                                                  \u00cd\\n  the resulting equilibrium has true regularised-CVaR welfare loss at most 2 \ud835\udc5b \u210e\ud835\udc5b , true unilateral regret\\n  at most 2\u210e\ud835\udc5b , and true participation shortfall at most 2\u210e\ud835\udc5b , subject to oracle-neighbourhood and multiplier\\n  assumptions. These are static guarantees; no sampled learning rule is established.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n1 The two source problems\\n  Garjani, Shokri and Kebriaei, denoted Q, consider \ud835\udc41 \u2265 2 agents choosing separate allocations\\n                                                                           \u00cd\\n  \ud835\udc65 \u2208 X\ud835\udc5b \u2286 \u211d+\ud835\udc3e and price messages \ud835\udc5d\ud835\udc5b \u2208 \u211d+\ud835\udc3e . Their planner maximises \ud835\udc5b \ud835\udc49\ud835\udc5b ( \ud835\udc65\ud835\udc5b ) subject to\\n  \u00cd\ud835\udc5b\\n     \ud835\udc5b \ud835\udc65 \ud835\udc5b \u2264 \ud835\udc50, with compact convex local sets and strongly concave valuations. Q proposes a\\n  quadratic payment, Eq. (40), and states unique Nash implementation, budget balance and\\n  individual rationality, together with a sample-based best-response convergence result under\\n  its stated conditions [3].\\n      Wang, Huang, Xu and Johansson, denoted P, instead minimise an average of local CVaR\\n  losses over one shared decision replicated across a network. They construct empirical CVaR\\n  estimates from sampled local losses and prove consensus and optimisation guarantees for\\n  that problem [5]. Its consensus iteration does not implement Q\u2019s heterogeneous allocation\\n  problem: even a two-agent quadratic example has optimal allocation (1, 0), while the equal\\n  allocation (1/2, 1/2) loses 1/4 of welfare. We use P\u2019s local value estimate, not its iteration.\\n\\n\\n\\n2 The Q payment: outcome and defect\\n                                        \u00cd                           \u00cd\\n  Write \ud835\udc61\ud835\udc5b for Q\u2019s Eq. (40), \ud835\udc67 =            \ud835\udc5a \ud835\udc65 \ud835\udc5a \u2212 \ud835\udc50, and \ud835\udc43 =          \ud835\udc5a \ud835\udc5d\ud835\udc5a . The own-price derivative of agent\\n  \ud835\udc5b\u2019s payoff is, coordinatewise,\\n                                                          \ud835\udefc\\n                                         \ud835\udf15 \ud835\udc5d\ud835\udc5b\ud835\udc58 \ud835\udc48\ud835\udc5b = \u2212        ( \ud835\udc41 \ud835\udc5d\ud835\udc5b\ud835\udc58 \u2212 \ud835\udc43 \ud835\udc58 \u2212 \ud835\udc67 \ud835\udc58 ) .\\n                                                        \ud835\udc41 \u22121\\n\\n\\n\\n                                                               1\\n\\f  Pathfinder paper                                                                                   Q7P1    \u00b7 2026-09-25\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n  At a price best response, if one price in coordinate \ud835\udc58 is positive, all prices there are positive\\n  and equal and \ud835\udc67 \ud835\udc58 = 0; if all are zero, the boundary conditions give \ud835\udc67 \ud835\udc58 \u2264 0. Thus every\\n  equilibrium has a common \ud835\udc5d \u2265 0, \ud835\udc67 \u2264 0, and \ud835\udc5d\u22a4 \ud835\udc67 = 0. At common prices, allocation best\\n  responses maximise \ud835\udc49\ud835\udc5b ( \ud835\udc65\ud835\udc5b ) \u2212 \ud835\udefc\ud835\udc5d\u22a4 \ud835\udc65\ud835\udc5b . These are the planner\u2019s optimality conditions with\\n  \ud835\udf06 = \ud835\udefc\ud835\udc5d, so every equilibrium allocation is optimal; strong concavity makes that allocation\\n  unique. Conversely, a planner primal-dual solution gives an equilibrium when a multiplier\\n  exists and Q\u2019s strong-concavity assumption makes each own payoff jointly concave in ( \ud835\udc65\ud835\udc5b , \ud835\udc5d\ud835\udc5b ).\\n  This conclusion concerns allocations, not uniqueness of price messages.\\n     Q\u2019s stated strict P1(ii) inequality cannot hold together with its P2(i) condition. The latter\\n            \u00cd\\n  requires \ud835\udc5a \ud835\udc34\ud835\udc5b\ud835\udc5b\ud835\udc5a = 0 for each \ud835\udc5b. On a direction changing all prices by the same nonzero\\n  vector \u210e, with allocations fixed, the quadratic form of Q\u2019s symmetric matrix bound is\\n                                                    \u2211\ufe01 \\u0010\u2211\ufe01      \\u0011\\n                               \ud835\udc63\u22a4 ( \u03a8 + \u03a8\u22a4 ) \ud835\udc63 = \u22122   \u210e\u22a4    \ud835\udc34\ud835\udc5b\ud835\udc5b\ud835\udc5a \u210e = 0,\\n                                                                 \ud835\udc5b       \ud835\udc5a\\n\\n  contrary to P1(ii)\u2019s strict negative bound. This disproves the proposed joint certificate, not\\n  implementation itself. Indeed, with \ud835\udc41 = 2, \ud835\udc50 = 2, X\ud835\udc5b = [0, 1], \ud835\udefc = 1, and \ud835\udc49\ud835\udc5b ( \ud835\udc65 ) = 2\ud835\udc65 \u2212 \ud835\udc65 2 /2,\\n  every common price \ud835\udc5d \u2208 [0, 1] supports the same equilibrium allocation (1, 1).\\n      The original payment can also violate participation. For \ud835\udc41 = 2, \ud835\udc50 = 1, X\ud835\udc5b = [0, 1], \ud835\udefc = 1,\\n  \ud835\udc491 ( \ud835\udc65 ) = 4 \ud835\udc65 \u2212 \ud835\udc65 2 /2 and \ud835\udc492 ( \ud835\udc65 ) = \ud835\udc65 \u2212 \ud835\udc65 2 /2, the message profile ( \ud835\udc651 , \ud835\udc5d1 , \ud835\udc652 , \ud835\udc5d2 ) = (1, 3, 0, 3)\\n  is an equilibrium. Q\u2019s payment charges agent 1 9/2, although its valuation is 7/2. This\\n  example can be realised with bounded convex stochastic losses compatible with P\u2019s local loss\\n  assumptions.\\n\\n\\n\\n3 An others-only transfer correction\\n                                \u00cd                           \u00cd\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_33\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/.local/bin/style-ban-artifacts paper/paper.tex paper/search.md && python3 /Users/v/.codex/skills/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py paper/paper.tex paper/search.md paper/paper.pdf'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"ite