t paper/paper.pdf - | tail -45\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"Pages:           5\\nFile size:       324232 bytes\\n  PATHFINDER PAPER \u00b7 Q7P1          \u00b7   2026-09-25\\n\\n\\n\\n  Balanced risk-aware allocation from an\\n  empirical CVaR oracle\\n  Q   A Systematic Approach to Mechanism Design with Stochastic Dynamic Stability                arXiv:2608.29130\\n\\n  P   Distributed risk-averse optimization via CVaR arXiv:2609.04460\\n\\n  Research phase: DRAFT after 2 rounds, 1 ITERATE, 0 REVISE. Edit phase: paper, review round 1\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n  Abstract\\n\\n  We examine the resource-allocation payment of Garjani, Shokri and Kebriaei together with the sampled\\n  conditional value at risk (CVaR) estimator of Wang, Huang, Xu and Johansson. The original payment\\n  implements an optimal allocation, but its stated strict uniqueness certificate is incompatible with its\\n  implementation condition, prices can be non-unique, and individual rationality can fail. An explicit\\n  transfer depending only on other agents\u2019 messages preserves the equilibrium set and gives exact budget\\n  balance and individual rationality at every equilibrium. For a fixed expected empirical-CVaR surrogate,\\n                                                                                  \u00cd\\n  the resulting equilibrium has true regularised-CVaR welfare loss at most 2 \ud835\udc5b \u210e\ud835\udc5b , true unilateral regret\\n  at most 2\u210e\ud835\udc5b , and true participation shortfall at most 2\u210e\ud835\udc5b , subject to oracle-neighbourhood and multiplier\\n  assumptions. These are static guarantees; no sampled learning rule is established.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n1 The two source problems\\n  Garjani, Shokri and Kebriaei, denoted Q, consider \ud835\udc41 \u2265 2 agents choosing separate allocations\\n                                                                           \u00cd\\n  \ud835\udc65 \u2208 X\ud835\udc5b \u2286 \u211d+\ud835\udc3e and price messages \ud835\udc5d\ud835\udc5b \u2208 \u211d+\ud835\udc3e . Their planner maximises \ud835\udc5b \ud835\udc49\ud835\udc5b ( \ud835\udc65\ud835\udc5b ) subject to\\n  \u00cd\ud835\udc5b\\n     \ud835\udc5b \ud835\udc65 \ud835\udc5b \u2264 \ud835\udc50, with compact convex local sets and strongly concave valuations. Q proposes a\\n  quadratic payment, Eq. (40), and states unique Nash implementation, budget balance and\\n  individual rationality, together with a sample-based best-response convergence result under\\n  its stated conditions [3].\\n      Wang, Huang, Xu and Johansson, denoted P, instead minimise an average of local CVaR\\n  losses over one shared decision replicated across a network. They construct empirical CVaR\\n  estimates from sampled local losses and prove consensus and optimisation guarantees for\\n  that problem [5]. Its consensus iteration does not implement Q\u2019s heterogeneous allocation\\n  problem: even a two-agent quadratic example has optimal allocation (1, 0), while the equal\\n  allocation (1/2, 1/2) loses 1/4 of welfare. We use P\u2019s local value estimate, not its iteration.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n                                                            1\\n\\f  Pathfinder paper                                                                                  Q7P1   \u00b7 2026-09-25\\n  are available. A surrogate planner multiplier is also required.\\n      The result is conditional on summing individual CVaRs and on quadratic regularisation;\\n  it gives no guarantee for the CVaR of total loss. It gives exact participation only for surrogate\\n  preferences and a 2\u210e\ud835\udc5b shortfall bound for true preferences. Q\u2019s numerical choice \ud835\udc44 \ud835\udc56 =\\n  \u23080.96\ud835\udc56+1 \u2309 equals one for every iteration as written, while its convergence theorem assumes\\n  geometrically increasing batches; those plots therefore do not verify the theorem\u2019s sampling\\n  regime. An implementable learning rule with feasible perturbations and convergence to the\\n  equilibrium set remains open.\\n\\n\\n\\n  References\\n  [1] Nathan Dahlin and Rahul Jain. A risk aware two-stage market mechanism for electricity\\n      with renewable generation, 2020. URL: https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.06119, arXiv:\\n      2003.06119.\\n\\n  [2] Kaixin Du and Min Meng. Distributed generalized nash equilibria learning for online\\n      stochastic aggregative games, 2025. URL: https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.06023, arXiv:\\n      2501.06023.\\n\\n  [3] Shaya Garjani, Mohammad Shokri, and Hamed Kebriaei. A systematic approach to\\n      mechanism design with stochastic dynamic stability, 2026. URL: https://arxiv.org/ab\\n      s/2608.29130, arXiv:2608.29130.\\n\\n  [4] Paul J. Healy and Laurent A. Mathevet. Designing stable mechanisms for economic\\n      environments. Theoretical Economics, 7(3):609\u2013661, 2012. URL: https://www.econtheo\\n      ry.org/ojs/index.php/te/article/viewArticle/20120609/240, doi:10.3982/TE898.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n                                                 4\\n\\fPathfinder paper                                                         Q7P1   \u00b7 2026-09-25\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n[5] Siyi Wang, Kun Huang, Lei Xu, and Karl H. Johansson. Distributed risk-averse optimiza-\\n    tion via CVaR, 2026. URL: https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.04460, arXiv:2609.04460.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n                                            5\\n\\f\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_44\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n '(\\\"'^!|LaTeX Warning|Package .* Warning|undefined|Overfull|Underfull|Citation)'\\\"' paper/paper.log paper/paper.blg || true; /Users/v/.local/bin/style-ban-artifacts paper/paper.tex paper/search.md paper/pathfinder-meta.tex; python3 /Users/v/.codex/skills/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py paper/paper.tex paper/search.md paper/pathfinder-meta.tex paper/paper.pdf | tail -3\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"WARN: /Users/v