f -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error paper.tex > /tmp/Q7P1-latexmk-final.log 2>&1; rc=$?; tail -12 /tmp/Q7P1-latexmk-final.log; pdfinfo paper.pdf | rg '\\\"'Pages:'; exit \\\"'$rc'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_47\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'latexmk -pdf -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error paper.tex > /tmp/Q7P1-latexmk-final.log 2>&1; rc=$?; tail -12 /tmp/Q7P1-latexmk-final.log; pdfinfo paper.pdf | rg '\\\"'Pages:'; exit \\\"'$rc'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"mf-dist/fonts/type1/public/newtx/txsys.pfb>\\nOutput written on paper.pdf (4 pages, 322708 bytes).\\nTranscript written on paper.log.\\nLatexmk: Getting log file 'paper.log'\\nLatexmk: Examining 'paper.fls'\\nLatexmk: Examining 'paper.log'\\nLatexmk: Found input bbl file 'paper.bbl'\\nLatexmk: Log file says output to 'paper.pdf'\\nLatexmk: Found bibliography file(s):\\n  ./references.bib\\nLatexmk: All targets () are up-to-date\\n\\nPages:           4\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_48\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"pdftoppm -f 4 -l 4 -scale-to 1200 -png -singlefile paper/paper.pdf /tmp/Q7P1-last-final && pdftotext -layout paper/paper.pdf - | tail -62 && rg -n '(\\\"'^!|LaTeX Warning|Package .* Warning|undefined|Overfull|Underfull|Citation)'\\\"' paper/paper.log paper/paper.blg || true\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_48\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"pdftoppm -f 4 -l 4 -scale-to 1200 -png -singlefile paper/paper.pdf /tmp/Q7P1-last-final && pdftotext -layout paper/paper.pdf - | tail -62 && rg -n '(\\\"'^!|LaTeX Warning|Package .* Warning|undefined|Overfull|Underfull|Citation)'\\\"' paper/paper.log paper/paper.blg || true\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"  allocation, its true utility differs from its nonnegative surrogate utility by at most 2\u210e\ud835\udc5b .        \u25a1\\n\\n\\n                                                                  3\\n\\f  Pathfinder paper                                                               Q7P1   \u00b7 2026-09-25\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n5 Relation to prior work\\n  The idea of repairing a mechanism\u2019s budget by adding terms that depend only on other agents\u2019\\n  messages already appears in Healy and Mathevet\u2019s study of stable Nash mechanisms [3].\\n  Our contribution is the explicit coefficient and equilibrium transfer identity for Q\u2019s particular\\n  quadratic payment, then the static value comparison using P\u2019s estimator; we do not claim\\n  the others-only device itself is new.\\n     Dahlin and Jain use CVaR in a two-stage electricity market but assume non-strategic\\n  agents for their market mechanism [1]. The targeted search recorded in search.md did not\\n  locate a publication of this precise Q-payment correction with P\u2019s empirical-CVaR bound; it\\n  cannot establish priority or novelty.\\n\\n\\n\\n6 Interpretation and limitations\\n  The theorem concerns equilibrium of a fixed expected empirical-CVaR surrogate, so its \ud835\udc60\ud835\udc5b\u22121/2\\n  term is a value-comparison guarantee rather than a convergence rate for sampled play. P\u2019s\\n  consensus algorithm addresses a shared decision, whereas Q has separate coupled allocations\\n  and possibly non-unique prices. Neither paper proves that a feasible learning rule reaches\\n  the corrected surrogate equilibrium from sampled losses. Q\u2019s allocation rule may violate\\n  capacity before equilibrium. Moreover, P\u2019s centred-ball feasible-set assumption cannot simply\\n  be imposed on Q\u2019s nonnegative sets; the theorem explicitly assumes all perturbation queries\\n  are available. A surrogate planner multiplier is also required.\\n      The result is conditional on summing individual CVaRs and on quadratic regularisation;\\n  it gives no guarantee for the CVaR of total loss. It gives exact participation only for surrogate\\n  preferences and a 2\u210e\ud835\udc5b shortfall bound for true preferences. Q\u2019s numerical choice \ud835\udc44 \ud835\udc56 =\\n  \u23080.96\ud835\udc56+1 \u2309 equals one for every iteration as written, while its convergence theorem assumes\\n  geometrically increasing batches; those plots therefore do not verify the theorem\u2019s sampling\\n  regime. An implementable learning rule with feasible perturbations and convergence to the\\n  equilibrium set remains open.\\n\\n\\n\\n  References\\n  [1] Nathan Dahlin and Rahul Jain. A risk aware two-stage market mechanism for electricity\\n      with renewable generation, 2020. URL: https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.06119, arXiv:\\n      2003.06119.\\n\\n  [2] Shaya Garjani, Mohammad Shokri, and Hamed Kebriaei. A systematic approach to\\n      mechanism design with stochastic dynamic stability, 2026. URL: https://arxiv.org/ab\\n      s/2608.29130, arXiv:2608.29130.\\n\\n  [3] Paul J. Healy and Laurent A. Mathevet. Designing stable mechanisms for economic\\n      environments. Theoretical Economics, 7(3):609\u2013661, 2012. URL: https://www.econtheo\\n      ry.org/ojs/index.php/te/article/viewArticle/20120609/240, doi:10.3982/TE898.\\n\\n  [4] Siyi Wang, Kun Huang, Lei Xu, and Karl H. Johansson. Distributed risk-averse optimiza-\\n      tion via CVaR, 2026. URL: https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.04460, arXiv:2609.04460.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n                                                 4\\n\\f\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_49\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'pdftoppm -f 1 -l 1 -scale-to 1200 -png -singlefile paper/paper.pdf /tmp/Q7P1-first-final'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\